 A Port is an interface we can provide for commands ("requests") over (Hypertext Transfer protocol)@https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html (HTTP). These consist of four main fields: a (universal resource locator)@https://url.spec.whatwg.org/ (URL) address, a method (mainly GET or PUT), a record of headers, and a stream of arbitrary data ("body"). To respond to this structure, we need to specify a context of a port number to receive them on, a domain name, certification and (distinguishedname)@https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4514 to "sign" our responses, and an actual "range" of responses to expose#b, which may demand additional syndication and encryption keys from the protocol specification (eg. API proxy or local password). With Interface we can supply these in modules to a command with their specifiers, like (expose)#script/highlight(`NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=./certificate_localhost.pem node --import=./interface.js ./port.js expose ./protocol.json/host/local ./static.js`){display:inline;word-break:break-all;white-space:normal;font-family:inherit}. 

