 After loading them, the expose#b command extracts the required protocol context, prompting the client for missing ones, and classifies#b the protocol, certificate and cache files to exclude them from the filesystem range. If a specified certification#b is not found, the distinguishedname#b is used to send a certificate signing request to Letsencrypt over ACME@https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8555/ protocol, or generate a self-signed one. To access the domain locally, the Fragment module's browser window "origin" is reassigned with the specified port for the reference of relative urls in fetch#b, which is why a certificate should be specified for localhost along with that of the public domain name, and assigned to NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS as it would be rejected as self-signed. When more certificates are provided, they are assigned to the host context#b to recognize their respective (domain name)#b in requests. 

 The domain module can define two ranges: the "default" passive http routes#b, and websocket "relay"#b actions to actively broadcast#b messages. A composition binds the agent#b interface with a respond#b function to a port, processing incoming requests by decoding#b their body, routing#b into the domain on their path, and persisting the described#b response#b into cache given no precedent was found there, that's otherwise reencoded#b for submission#b. 

